# tools/al/ales_power_witness.rish — equal-power pan: the law that keeps POWER constant, not SUM (ALES11).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES11.
# Design read: active-designing/20260814-fill-ales11-lotus-equal-power.md
# Stands on: lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) — the second channel (StereoClip) and the fold bound (max_pan_den) reused whole;
# lotus/fader.rye (ALES8) — a panned track is TWO faded contributions, so each channel is fader.render run once over an
# equal-power-folded effective fader; lotus/track.rye (ALES6) — the Session the pans align with.
#
# lotus/power.rye places each track on the QUARTER CIRCLE rather than the straight line between the speakers, so the two
# channels' POWERS (their squared amplitudes) sum to a constant. "Equal power" MEANS the two squared weights sum to den² —
# built exact from that fact (left_power = den·(den−pos), right_power = den·pos), the amplitude weights the floor square root
# of each. The one genuinely new arithmetic is a bounded integer sqrt, proven to its contract r² ≤ x < (r+1)². The selftest
# proves: the isqrt contract across a sweep; constant power for every position; a center pan at 0.707 (−3 dB) distinctly
# louder than ALES10's linear 0.5 (−6 dB); hard left/right route wholly; left²+right² within the proven bound of den²; pan
# composes with the fader; two tracks panned opposite separate; make refuses BadGain/BadRange; ClipFull forwarded. Purely
# local — no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/al/ales_power_witness.rish

say "ales-power: Language EN — ALES11 places each track on the quarter circle, so the two channels' powers sum to a constant: constant loudness where the linear law dipped −6 dB at center."
say "ales-power: Style Radiant — the law is stated positively: left_power + right_power = den² exactly, and the amplitude weights are the floor square roots that bend the straight power line into the circle the ear hears."
say "ales-power: Lens TAME — the one new arithmetic is a bounded integer sqrt with a proven contract (r² ≤ x < (r+1)²); the pan fold feeds it inputs ≤ 2²⁴, and the root itself is bounded to the audio square domain 2³⁰ so ALES13's meter shares the one root; each channel is ALES8's fader.render run once over an equal-power fold; refuses BadGain / BadRange / ClipFull by name; siloed to lotus/, no network, no keys, no funds, no device."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/power"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/power.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-power: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/power.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-power: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-power: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-power' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-power: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-power line"

say "ales-power: GREEN — the equal-power law keeps power constant where the linear law kept sum; the bounded isqrt is proven to its contract, and a center pan holds 0.707 (−3 dB) where ALES10 gave 0.5 (−6 dB). The next Lotus rung can name the equal-power crossfade over time, stereo metering (an RMS is a square root), or offer the keeper the choice of both laws."
